OL,2,W//%*// //%Q-.*,%-,* *,%%**,$ ,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,AHow 'mogging' went from toxic term to
OL,4,Amainstream
OL,5,
OL,6,FThe word is defined by Collins as being
OL,7,Fused playfully to mean "the act of
OL,8,Foutshining or outperforming someone or
OL,9,Fsomething" and is now widely employed
OL,10,Fin online discourse about celebrity
OL,11,Fculture, with signs it is making its
OL,12,Fway into everyday vocabulary.
OL,14,F"I heard someone the other day say they
OL,15,Fwere 'walk-mogged' - basically just
OL,16,Fsomeone else in the street about their
OL,17,Fage walking faster than them," says
OL,18,FTony Thorne, a language consultant at
OL,19,FKing's College London.
OL,23,APub: 03-NovQppppppppppppppppppppppG2/2
